<p>An underpaid insurance claim in Corpus Christi usually does not look like a dispute at first. The insurer accepts the claim, sends an estimate, and issues a payment. Then the roofer's bid comes in at twice the amount, or the drywall contractor points out rooms that were never included. Underpayment is one of the most common problems property owners bring to a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster, and it can often be traced to specific, fixable gaps in the insurer's estimate.</p>

<h2>Why Property Claims Come In Low</h2>
<p>Most insurer estimates are written in Xactimate, the same software we use. The software is not the issue; the inputs are. Underpayments typically come from one or more of these sources:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Missing scope.</strong> Rooms, roof slopes, elevations, or entire structures such as detached garages and fences are left out.</li>
<li><strong>Low quantities.</strong> Roof squares, wall square footage, or linear feet of trim are undercounted, or waste factors are too low.</li>
<li><strong>Repair instead of replace.</strong> The estimate patches a few areas when the damage, the materials, or the code calls for replacement.</li>
<li><strong>Omitted components.</strong> Underlayment, drip edge, starter strip, flashing, ridge vent, and code-required items are left off.</li>
<li><strong>Pricing that does not reflect the market.</strong> After a large storm, local labor and material costs can rise, and default pricing may lag.</li>
<li><strong>Missing general contractor overhead and profit</strong> on jobs that require coordinating several trades, where the policy and facts support it.</li>
<li><strong>Heavy depreciation</strong> on items with years of useful life left, or age assumptions that do not match the property records.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our article on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a> walks through these items with examples.</p>

<h2>How the Payment Is Calculated</h2>
<p>Understanding the math helps you spot where money is being held back. A typical property payment follows this sequence, although your policy controls the details.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th><th>Where underpayment can occur</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1. Replacement cost value (RCV)</td><td>The estimated cost to repair or replace the damage with like kind and quality</td><td>Missing scope, low quantities, low pricing</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Less depreciation</td><td>Subtracted to reach actual cash value (ACV)</td><td>Excessive depreciation rates or age assumptions</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Less deductible</td><td>Your deductible is applied</td><td>Wrong deductible applied, such as a windstorm percentage deductible on a non-wind loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Less prior payments</td><td>Earlier checks are credited</td><td>Prior payments counted twice or not documented</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Recoverable depreciation</td><td>Released after repairs under many RCV policies, within a policy deadline</td><td>Deadlines missed, or repair invoices not submitted</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Many Texas coastal policies carry a separate windstorm, hail, or named-storm deductible, often stated as a percentage of the dwelling limit. Our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> shows how a percentage deductible translates into dollars, and the <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> shows how depreciation and prior payments affect the net check. For background, read <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</p>

