<h2>Coastal Bend Factors That Affect Estimates</h2>
<p>Local conditions can widen the gap between an insurer's estimate and the real cost of repair in Corpus Christi and nearby towns:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Windstorm certification.</strong> Roof repairs and re-roofs in the TDI catastrophe area involve windstorm inspection and certification, which can affect materials, fastening, and cost.</li>
<li><strong>City permits and codes.</strong> Corpus Christi requires a permit for re-roofing, and the city's submission includes a TDI WPI-1 form. The city's local design criteria list a 140/150 mph design wind speed and a windborne debris zone. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Demand surges after big storms.</strong> After events like Hurricane Harvey or the May 8, 2025 windstorm, contractor availability and pricing can change quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Salt-air and humidity.</strong> Insurers may attribute rust or deterioration to age. Separating storm damage from long-term wear takes careful documentation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Signs Your Claim May Be Underpaid</h2>
<p>You do not need to be an estimator to notice warning signs. Any of these is worth a closer look:</p>
<ul>
<li>Licensed contractors will not do the work for the amount in the estimate.</li>
<li>The estimate lists fewer rooms, slopes, or structures than you know were damaged.</li>
<li>Interior ceiling stains appear in the photos but not in the line items.</li>
<li>The estimate repairs one section of flooring or cabinets that cannot be matched to the rest.</li>
<li>Contents, debris removal, or additional living expense were never addressed.</li>
<li>The adjuster spent a short time at the property after a large storm, when inspections are often rushed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Contents and Living Expenses Are Often Overlooked</h2>
<p>Building estimates get most of the attention, but underpayment frequently shows up in other parts of the claim. Personal property may be valued with heavy depreciation or left off entirely when owners have not prepared an inventory. Additional living expense, often called loss of use, may not be requested at all, even when the home was unlivable for weeks while drying and repairs took place. On business policies, business income and extra expense can be larger than the building claim. Each of these coverages has its own limits and conditions, so review your declarations page and keep receipts. A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can help organize inventories and expense records so they can be presented alongside the building claim.</p>

<h2>What You Can Do About an Underpaid Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get the full estimate,</strong> not just the summary page. Line items show exactly what was and was not included.</li>
<li><strong>Collect written bids</strong> from licensed local contractors that describe the same scope in detail.</li>
<li><strong>Document anything new.</strong> Damage found during tear-off or demolition should be photographed and reported before it is covered up.</li>
<li><strong>Send a written supplement or reconsideration request</strong> with your supporting documents. See <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Watch deadlines,</strong> including the deadline to recover depreciation and any TWIA appraisal window.</li>
<li><strong>Consider appraisal</strong> if coverage is agreed and only the amount is disputed. Our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page explains how it works.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How We Help With Underpaid Claims</h2>
<p>We begin with a line-by-line <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>, comparing the insurer's estimate with the property and with your policy. We reinspect the damage, measure, photograph, and use drone or Matterport imaging when it helps the insurer see what we see. Then we prepare our own detailed estimate and a written explanation of each difference, and we present it to the insurer and follow up through reinspection and negotiation.</p>
<p>Not every gap is recoverable. Some differences come from policy limits, exclusions, or depreciation terms that are applied correctly, and we will tell you when that is the case. We cannot promise a higher payment. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> publishes consumer guidance on property claims and accepts complaints about insurers. If you think your settlement falls short, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> and we will look at the estimate with you.</p>
